622575547.en:
A year ago, we were very close to stabilizing the very useful `breakpoint`  intrinsic. Then we realized that different people had subtly different ideas about what the semantics of the intrinsic even should be, and specifically, whether code like this ought to be sound:
```rust
pub fn my_fun() {
  breakpoint();
  unsafe { std::hint::unreachable_unchecked() };
}
```
It took  a lot of energy to get to the bottom of what we all even had in mind as semantics, which culminated in [this great summary](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust/issues/133724#issuecomment-3055163595). And then... discussion stopped.

Is there any appetite in t-libs to pick this back up, choose one of these two semantics (`yeet1` or `yeet2` -- since `trap` is clearly different, it has a different type signature), and bless them as `breakpoint`, with the possibility of adding the other option under a less prominent name later should it turn out to be useful?

