621487004.en:
I wondered whether IO (in particular the input part) gives some angelic choice in our semantics. For example, take this pseudo-code:
```
fn f1() {
  let demonic = freeze(poison);
  let user = read();
  if demonic != user { unreachable(); }
}
```
Is the UB avoidable by the user input or not? I.e., is this program equivalent to
```
fn f2() {
  let demonic = freeze(poison);
  let user = read();
  unreachable();
}
```
? If yes, that seems surprising. But clearly
```
fn f3() {
  let user = read();
  let demonic = freeze(poison);
  if demonic != user { unreachable(); }
}
```
is equivalent to `f2`. So you can't move a freeze down across IO?

