622017933.en:
@_**niluxv|401145** [said](https://rust-lang.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/136281-t-opsem/topic/Do.20we.20already.20have.20overlapping.20allocations.3F.20.28OOM.20Edition.29/near/621933677):
```quote
> instead of allocation planes I just imagine all allocations having unique IDs

~~Yes, that is equivalent (but you need to fix a bijection between AllocIds and rationals except 0, to use for order comparisons).~~ Edit: with comparisons it can actually make a difference.
```

Ugh, with comparisons everything becomes subtle. I suppose we order anti-lexicographically, so first compare the plane, then the address. This feels more intuitive since if you take two pointers in the same allocation, there won't be pointers into a different allocation in-between.

The model which allows multiple allocations to live in the same plane allows strictly more behaviours than the one where there is at most 1 allocation per plane except for plane 0:

```rust
fn exhaust_plane_0() {
    let layout = Layout::new(1, 1);
    for i in 0..usize::MAX {
        let ptr: *mut u8 = alloc(layout).expose_provenance();
        assert!(!x.is_null());
    }
}

fn main() {
    exhaust_plane_0();
    let ptr1 = alloc();
    let plane1_low = ptr1.with_addr(0);
    let plane1_high = ptr1.with_addr(usize::MAX);
    let ptr2 = alloc();
    // can this print `true`?
    println!(ptr1_low <= ptr2 && ptr2 <= ptr1_high);
}
```

With multiple allocations per plane you could see the program print `true`. With one allocation per plane (the AllocId + forced bit approach) that can never happen.

