622020377.en:
Let me then also give the example which shows the difference between integer and rational planes.

```rust
fn main() {
    let mut ptr1 = alloc();
    let mut ptr2 = alloc();
    // sort the pointers
    if ptr2 < ptr1 {
        (ptr1, ptr2) = (ptr2, ptr1);
    }

    loop {
        let ptr = alloc();
        if ptr1 < ptr && ptr < ptr2 {
            dealloc(ptr2);
            ptr2 = ptr;
        } else {
            // is this guaranteed to be reached (if no OOM occurs)?
            println!("hello!");
        }
    }
}
```

Assuming no OOM occurs, the integer plane semantics guarantees that we eventually get to the println. The rational plane semantics does not guarantee this, and in fact the whole function can be optimized to just `loop {}` in that case.

